 "id": "2023-rui-which",
 "name": "Which Street Landscape Looks Better? (Fuzhou)",
 "description": "Method replication: participants see two street views from the main urban area of Fuzhou and answer the paper's question, \"Which image has a better visual effect on the street landscape?\", choosing either image or \"About the same\" (the paper's >, < and =), for 30 pairs. The choices can be turned into Elo scores as in the paper. The images are not the paper's stimuli (49 Baidu Street View forward views of the Gulou, Taijiang, Cangshan and Jin'an districts, 2016–2023, cropped to remove the capture car).\nOriginal study: Rui & Cheng (2023), \"Quantifying the spatial quality of urban streets with open street view images: A case study of the main urban area of Fuzhou\", Ecological Indicators 156, 111204. 500 of 24,636 Baidu sample points were compared in pairs by two groups, city residents and landscape architects; the Elo scores (starting at 1,000 points, averaged over the two groups) trained a random forest on segmentation indicators (green view, sky view, enclosure, colour richness, visual entropy and others) that mapped visual street quality across the city.\nDifferences from the original study: (1) 30 pairs per participant from 49 new images instead of the paper's 500 sample points; (2) one forward view per point instead of four directions; (3) participants say whether they are residents or landscape architects instead of being recruited as two separate groups; (4) gender is asked.",
